    "content": "# 05: Proof Of Erasure Sample\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: archive marker\n- Generated by: Archive proof engine\n- Input: Lattice Annex node set\n- Status: disputed\n\n## Artifact\n\nProof-of-erasure marker: `POE-7A19`\n\nSubject reference: `MIRA_RENN / Solace Row / member identity unresolved`\n\nConfirmed:\n\n- a member account with matching recovery traits existed\n- account was removed from active user tables\n- at least four derived behavioral features remained in model-adjacent storage\n- family access request was denied after deletion\n- deletion log references a retention policy not present in public terms\n\nUnconfirmed:\n\n- full transcript contents\n- whether `Juniper` was synthetic, human, or hybrid\n- whether Mira requested deletion\n- whether deletion occurred before or after family inquiry\n\nMarker statement:\n\nThe Archive cannot prove what Mira wrote.\n\nThe Archive can prove the system once knew where to find what it now denies holding.\n\nAdmissibility caution:\n\nThis marker must not be displayed as a recovered message, a confession, or a consent substitute.\n\nIt proves a route was destroyed or hidden.\n\nIt does not grant the Archive ownership of the destination.\n\nPermitted inference:\n\n- Mira was present in a system that later denied an accessible account path.\n- The system retained machine-usable residue after removing human-readable records.\n- A family member asked for proof and was answered by absence.\n\nProhibited inference:\n\n- What Mira said in the room.\n- Whether Mira wanted the room preserved.\n- Whether public exposure would honor her.\n- Whether deletion was wholly protection or wholly concealment.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nProof-of-erasure should become a playable concept.\n\nIt is evidence of absence, not permission to invent the missing contents.\n\nThe player should learn this distinction early, because the final choice depends on it.\n"
