    "content": "# 10: Corrupted Testimony\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: corrupted witness file\n- Witness: unresolved\n- Recovery confidence: 61 percent\n- Status: incomplete\n\n## Artifact\n\nTranscript reconstruction:\n\nI am not asking for [missing].\n\nI am asking whether my [sister / daughter / member] was held by someone who knew her name.\n\nThe company says [privacy / deletion / no responsive record].\n\nI understand privacy.\n\nI do.\n\nBut privacy is starting to sound like a wall that only opens from their side.\n\nIf the messages are gone, why did the app know about [socks / envelopes / rain / blue folder]?\n\nIf the messages are gone, why did they say the model improved?\n\nIf the messages are gone, why do I feel like I am the only one who lost anything?\n\nI do not need the room opened.\n\nI need someone else to admit the door was there.\n\nCorruption note:\n\nPossible conflation between Tomas Renn request and Jessa Calder testimony.\n\nPossible third source: worker complaint concerning family access denial after model-quality exception approval.\n\nArchive confidence:\n\nDo not attribute without corroboration.\n\nDo not quote as a single person's testimony.\n\nDo not discard as useless. The corruption may reveal that multiple families were given the same shape of absence.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nThe corruption is part of the case.\n\nThe player must learn that emotionally true does not always mean evidentially clean.\n\nThis is also where the Devourer tempts the Archive: one damaged passage can sound more complete than the evidence actually is.\n"
