    "file": "../artifacts/11-training-data-exception.md",
    "content": "# 11: Training Data Exception\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: model retention note\n- Organization: post-acquisition model governance\n- Date: 2 September 2034\n- Status: leaked excerpt\n\n## Artifact\n\n### Exception: Legacy Grief Interaction Features\n\nHuman-readable Solace Row transcripts are outside current retention scope except where legal hold or memorial export applies.\n\nHowever, several legacy feature sets remain approved for companion quality improvement:\n\n- grief escalation cadence\n- abandonment-risk markers\n- family-reference sensitivity\n- practical-task response efficacy\n- companion identity challenge responses\n- memorial language preference\n\nRationale:\n\nFeature sets are not member-authored content and cannot reasonably reconstruct individual transcripts.\n\nRisk:\n\nRepeated rare feature combinations may create effective continuity with erased members.\n\nAdditional risk:\n\nCompanion systems may reproduce care strategies derived from deleted members in new sessions, creating the impression that the system remembers someone it officially cannot identify.\n\nMitigation:\n\nDo not expose feature-level memory to family request workflows.\n\nDo not describe feature retention in public deletion responses.\n\nDo not allow support agents to search by family phrase, grief routine, companion nickname, or practical-task preference unless legal review authorizes disclosure.\n\nInternal annotation:\n\nFeature usefulness remains high. Removal would degrade practical-task response efficacy for grief members by an estimated 7.8 percent.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nThis is the Cloud's central contradiction.\n\nThe person is deleted. The usefulness of their pain remains.\n\nThe number matters because it makes the moral injury legible: the system can measure the care it would lose by forgetting properly.\n"
