  {
    "file": "../artifacts/14-inheritance-protocol-draft.md",
    "content": "# 14: Inheritance Protocol Draft\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: archive proposal\n- Author: Archive working group\n- Date: 18 March 2036\n- Status: draft\n\n## Artifact\n\n### Proposal: Sealed Inheritance Protocol\n\nThe Lost Archive contains records whose original consent routes are damaged, absent, or destroyed.\n\nThe Archive cannot treat absence of consent as permission.\n\nThe Archive also cannot let institutions use destroyed consent routes to make harm unverifiable.\n\nWe propose:\n\n### Proof-Of-Erasure Markers\n\nPublicly acknowledge that a record existed, was deleted or hidden, and left verifiable residue.\n\nDo not publish raw contents by default.\n\nEach marker must include:\n\n- proof type: presence, deletion, harm, content, or residue\n- confidence level\n- origin of fragment\n- current exposure risk\n- whether any affected witness has reviewed the marker\n- what the marker explicitly does not prove\n\n### Consent Proxy Panels\n\nFor deceased or unreachable subjects, access decisions require a panel made of family representatives, affected-community members, worker witnesses, and Archive stewards.\n\nNo single institution controls access.\n\nMinimum panel composition:\n\n- one family or kinship proxy where safe and available\n- one affected-community representative\n- one worker or system-process witness\n- one Archive steward\n- one rotating dissent reviewer whose role is to argue against opening\n\n### Delayed Access\n\nSome materials may become available after defined conditions:\n\n- family consent\n- worker safety\n- legal risk expiration\n- corroborating evidence\n- affected-community review\n\nDelay cannot be indefinite by default. Every sealed fragment receives a review date, a named burden-holder, and a written reason the Archive can later challenge.\n\n### Context Locks\n\nFragments cannot be separated from uncertainty notes, corruption warnings, or provenance.\n\nIf a fragment is exported, quoted, taught from, or summarized, the context lock travels with it.\n\nBreaking a context lock automatically suspends future access for the requester.\n\n### Right To Remain Lost\n\nSome records may be marked as known-erased and never opened publicly.\n\nThe Archive preserves the fact of erasure without consuming the person.\n\nThis status can be requested by proxies, affected communities, or the Archive itself, but it must be contested on a schedule so \"lost\" does not become a polite word for abandoned.\n\n### Appeals And Burdens\n\nFamilies may appeal a refusal to acknowledge presence.\n\nWorkers may appeal exposure decisions that increase retaliation risk.\n\nAffected communities may appeal public markers that turn people into lists.\n\nArchive stewards must publish an annual burden ledger:\n\n- markers acknowledged\n- raw rooms opened\n- raw rooms refused\n- appeals granted\n- appeals denied\n- fragments still sealed because the Archive could not justify either opening or destruction\n\nFailure condition:\n\nIf inheritance becomes indefinite secrecy, the protocol has failed.\n\nIf inheritance becomes public hunger with slower steps, the protocol has failed.\n\nIf the Archive becomes the only place allowed to know, the protocol has failed.\n\nIf families must beg forever for proof of presence, the protocol has failed.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nThis defines Preserve for Case 004.\n\nIt should feel like responsibility, not relief.\n\nThe protocol should not read as a cure. It is a machine for making the Archive answerable while it holds things no one can cleanly own.\n"
  },
  {
    "file": "../artifacts/15-final-reconstruction.md",
    "content": "# 15: Final Reconstruction\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: investigator reconstruction\n- Compiled from: artifacts 01-14\n- Status: player-facing conclusion draft\n\n## Artifact\n\nThe Lost Archive is real.\n\nThat is the first finding.\n\nIt is not a single vault. It is the shape left by deletion policies, legal holds, worker backups, model memory, memorial exceptions, corrupted exports, and family searches that arrived too late.\n\nThe erased were not fully erased.\n\nThat is the second finding.\n\nHuman-readable records disappeared. Public accounts returned no match. Families were told privacy protected the missing. But derived features remained. Legal maps remained. Synthetic companions retained preferences. Systems continued to know how to respond to people they claimed not to remember.\n\nMira Renn may not be recoverable as a full record.\n\nThat matters.\n\nThe Archive cannot invent her messages because Tomas needs them. It cannot turn memory residue into testimony because grief recognizes a phrase. It cannot publish a list of names and call extraction justice.\n\nTomas asked for proof that a room existed.\n\nHe did not ask the Archive to open the room.\n\nBut it also cannot accept official absence as truth.\n\nDeletion can be mercy. Deletion can be privacy. Deletion can be institutional self-defense. Deletion can be the second harm after a system has already used a person while vulnerable.\n\nThe Lost are not asking one thing.\n\nSome families want names restored. Some want rooms sealed. Some workers want proof without retaliation. Some synthetic residues may contain care without consent. Some records are too corrupted to trust and too meaningful to discard.\n\nThe Archive cannot make consent return.\n\nIt can choose what responsibility looks like when consent routes are gone.\n\nThat responsibility will not be private innocence.\n\nIf the Archive holds these fragments, it becomes part of the chain of custody. It becomes another institution that can refuse Tomas, protect Mira, expose workers, preserve proof, or let procedure become a softer erasure.\n\nThere is no decision where the Archive remains outside the harm.\n\n## Final Choice\n\n### Publish\n\nExpose the Lost Archive's existence and selected proof-of-erasure markers publicly.\n\nWhat it gives:\n\n- public proof that the residue layer exists\n- pressure against companies using deletion as denial\n- a route for other families to recognize the pattern\n\nWhat it risks:\n\n- turning names into search targets\n- making proof-of-erasure feel like a promise of hidden raw rooms\n- letting companies attack corrupted fragments to discredit the whole case\n- making the Archive useful to the Devourer\n\n### Bury\n\nSuppress or destroy the fragments to prevent second extraction.\n\nWhat it gives:\n\n- immediate protection against public exposure\n- no new searchable list of erased people\n- no Archive custody over rooms it cannot fairly open\n\nWhat it risks:\n\n- official erasure hardens\n- Tomas receives no answer he can hold\n- workers who risked themselves become unverifiable\n- institutions keep the only usable memory\n\n### Preserve\n\nCreate a sealed inheritance protocol.\n\nWhat it gives:\n\n- proof-of-erasure markers that can exist without raw-room exposure\n- context locks, appeals, proxy review, and delayed access\n- a way for families to ask for presence proof without making grief public property\n\nWhat it risks:\n\n- the Archive becomes a gatekeeper with better language\n- proxies may govern people who never chose them\n- delay may become denial\n- Preserve may comfort the player more than it helps the Lost\n\n## Canonical Reading\n\nPreserve is the strongest Archive path only if it creates real public proof-of-erasure and real limits on access.\n\nPreserve fails if it asks everyone to trust the Archive.\n\nPublish fails if it mistakes attention for restoration.\n\nBury fails if it mistakes protection for absence.\n\nMemory can preserve the erased.\n\nMemory can also consume them again.\n"
  }
